{
  "difficulty": "hard",
  "score": 7,
  "estimated_minutes": 65,
  "factors": {
    "transaction_background": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The reviewer must trace St. Jude’s transfer of the Campana Construct, Penn’s creation of the June Construct, and the shift from preclinical research under the 2003 MTA to clinical trials under the 2007 MTA."
    },
    "domain_knowledge": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "Understanding 'portions' and 'unmodified derivatives' requires grasping how PCR copies a DNA sequence without transferring the original physical material and how Penn modified that copy for a lentiviral vector."
    },
    "defect_spread": {
      "level": "high",
      "note": "The meaning of 'Material(s)' must be read across both MTAs’ definitions, the 2003 commercialization restriction, and the 2007 permitted-purpose and patent/commercialization provisions."
    },
    "court_reasoning": {
      "level": "medium",
      "note": "The court clearly finds facial ambiguity but treats the provisions collectively, requiring the reviewer to distinguish that limited ruling from Penn’s supporting arguments and the unresolved course-of-performance and trade-usage evidence."
    },
    "issue_distinctness": {
      "level": "high",
      "note": "All listed defects share lines 107–111, and the definition, 'contains' restriction, 'incorporating' purpose language, and derivative-based notice triggers are overlapping applications of the same physical-material-versus-copied-sequence dispute."
    }
  },
  "hardest_defect": "d05 — The reviewer must distinguish the three alternative notice triggers from the definition of 'Materials' and the separate commercialization restriction. The court accepts competing overall readings as reasonable without separately explaining how 'derived from' or 'could not have been produced but for' applies to the June Construct.",
  "reason": "Assign this to a reviewer comfortable with technical facts and closely related contractual issues. The scientific distinction between copying a sequence and incorporating transferred physical material is essential, although the opinion supplies enough background without requiring patent-law expertise. The main difficulty is mapping the court’s collective ambiguity ruling onto distinct provisions without treating every supporting argument as a separately adopted construction. Targeted rereading of the scientific account, both MTAs, and the summary-judgment analysis should suffice; the willful-infringement and jury-trial discussions are not necessary to understand these defects."
}
